ML_PAGE("t3-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l3\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 3 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the case, evaluate the trade-off, recommend. The answer technique and model answers are below.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l3\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The 5-move ERM answer, tuned for Topic 3</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Define</b><p>Governance, risk governance, SOX, COSO or ISO precisely, including its nature (law, framework, standard, code).</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the problem it solves</b><p>Oversight, information asymmetry, rules of the game, accountability.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply</b><p>Map the case to the five elements or the eight COSO components.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate</b><p>Is compliance enough? Do controls guarantee zero risk? Is the board informed?</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>5. Recommend</b><p>Concrete governance and control fixes.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Turn scandals into governance analysis: which mechanism should have prevented, detected or escalated the problem?</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"The board should not approve because it is risky.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Everything interesting is risky; no governance reasoning.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"The board should not approve the acquisition in its current information state, because material conflicts of interest, inadequate due diligence, cybersecurity exposure and debt risk prevent a sufficiently informed decision. It should require independent due diligence, conflict management, a revised valuation, debt stress testing and a formal risk report before reconsidering.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Conditional judgement + named failures + specific remedies.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Distinguish management from governance with a banking example. (6 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t3-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t3-1\">Management runs daily operations: in a bank it decides how many loans to approve, how branches operate and how staff are allocated. Governance is the system of laws, rules, processes and ethical structures by which the firm is directed and controlled: who may approve large loans, how much credit risk is acceptable, who reviews management and what information must reach the board. Governance sits one level above execution, but the two interact: weak governance permits excessive risk-taking that damages performance and investor confidence.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain the five elements of corporate governance. (10 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t3-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t3-2\">Accountability matches authority with responsibility so someone answers for decisions and outcomes. Transparency ensures relevant information reaches those who need it, since decision quality cannot exceed information quality. The regulatory framework sets external rules and minimum expectations. Business ethics and social responsibility address conduct that is legal but wrong (compliance asks \"can we?\", ethics asks \"should we?\"). Administrative structure defines who owns, monitors, approves, receives reports on and escalates each risk. Each solves a different governance problem.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Compare SOX, COSO and ISO 31000. (9 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t3-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t3-3\">SOX (2002) is US law enacted after Enron and WorldCom to protect investors by improving the accuracy and reliability of disclosures and deterring fraud; it focuses on accountability, internal controls and reporting. COSO is an ERM framework structured as a cube of objectives (strategic, operations, reporting, compliance), eight components and organisational levels. ISO 31000 is a voluntary standard, not for certification, made up of principles, a framework and a process, emphasising risk management as part of decision making. They differ in nature (law, framework, standard) but overlap in purpose.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">APPLY</span>\n        <h4>Apply the COSO components to a rushed, conflicted RM3bn acquisition. (12 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t3-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t3-4\">Internal environment: directors with ties to the target and a dominant CEO suggest a culture where management is not challenged. Objective setting: the value the acquisition must create is not clearly defined. Event identification: cyber weaknesses, integration failure, debt pressure. Risk assessment: likelihood and impact were not analysed because due diligence was rushed. Risk response: reduce debt, renegotiate price, strengthen cyber controls or abandon. Control activities: independent due diligence, conflict declarations, approval thresholds. Information and communication: the risk team's concerns did not reach the board in full. Monitoring: post-acquisition performance should be tracked.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EVALUATE</span>\n        <h4>\"Regulatory compliance demonstrates strong ERM.\" Evaluate. (8 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t3-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t3-5\">Disagree. Compliance satisfies minimum legal obligations, such as correct disclosure and approval procedures, and is necessary. But a firm can be fully compliant while making a value-destroying strategic decision, such as overpaying for an acquisition, or ignoring operational and emerging risks. Controls also reduce rather than eliminate risk. Strong ERM additionally requires risk embedded in decision making, board oversight, and management of strategic and emerging risks. Compliance is necessary but not sufficient.</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Practice questions from the notes</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P1</span><span>Define corporate governance and differentiate it from management. <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define corporate governance and differentiate it from management. Explain why the distinction is important for effective organisational risk management.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Corporate governance refers to the structures, policies, laws and processes through which an organisation is directed, administered and controlled in the interests of shareholders and other stakeholders.</p>\n<p>Its primary objective is to enhance shareholder value while considering the interests of other stakeholders.</p>\n<p>Management is primarily responsible for conducting the organisation's daily activities, implementing strategies and allocating resources to achieve operational objectives.</p>\n<p>Corporate governance, on the other hand, establishes the overall system of direction, oversight, accountability and control within which management operates.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturing company's management may decide how to schedule production, purchase materials and maintain equipment.</p>\n<p>The board of directors, through its governance responsibilities, oversees the company's strategy, major risks and adequacy of relevant control systems.</p>\n<p>This distinction is important because management should not operate without appropriate oversight. Excessive concentration of decision-making authority without accountability may increase the likelihood of poor decisions, conflicts of interest or inadequate risk management.</p>\n<p>Conversely, governance that interferes excessively in routine operations may reduce management efficiency.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective corporate governance requires a clear separation of responsibilities, reliable reporting and sufficient oversight while allowing management to execute business activities efficiently.</p>\n<p>The lecture distinguishes daily management from governance and explains that weak governance can undermine operating performance and investor confidence.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition of corporate governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explanation of management</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clear distinction between the two</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevant application</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis of consequences and importance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The deeper lesson</h3>\n<p>A company may have highly capable managers but still suffer serious governance failures.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Management Capability}\\neq\\text{Governance Quality}}$$</div>\n<p>Someone can be excellent at increasing revenue but poor at managing conflicts of interest, disclosing risks or respecting oversight.</p>\n<p>For a high-scoring answer, discuss the allocation of authority and accountability, not simply the organisational hierarchy.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P2</span><span>Explain the five elements of corporate governance and critically discuss how weaknesses in these elements may expose... <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the five elements of corporate governance and critically discuss how weaknesses in these elements may expose an organisation to risk.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies accountability, transparency, regulatory framework, business ethics and social responsibility, and administrative structure as its five elements.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Effective corporate governance depends on several interconnected elements that promote responsible management, transparency and organisational accountability.</p>\n<p>1. Accountability</p>\n<p>Accountability ensures that individuals and organisational bodies are answerable for their decisions, responsibilities and performance.</p>\n<p>For example, senior management should be required to explain major investment decisions and their associated risks to the board.</p>\n<p>Without accountability, managers may undertake excessive risks or make decisions without facing appropriate scrutiny.</p>\n<p>2. Transparency</p>\n<p>Transparency refers to the appropriate disclosure and communication of relevant organisational information to stakeholders and decision-makers.</p>\n<p>For example, a listed company should provide reliable information concerning financial performance and material exposures.</p>\n<p>Weak transparency may prevent investors and directors from assessing the company's actual position, potentially reducing investor confidence.</p>\n<p>3. Regulatory framework</p>\n<p>The regulatory framework consists of the relevant laws, rules and formal requirements governing organisational activities.</p>\n<p>Compliance with these requirements helps establish minimum standards of acceptable corporate conduct.</p>\n<p>Failure to comply may result in regulatory action, legal liabilities and reputational damage.</p>\n<p>4. Business ethics and social responsibility</p>\n<p>This element encourages organisations to act responsibly towards employees, customers, shareholders and other stakeholders.</p>\n<p>It goes beyond simply complying with legal requirements by considering whether business conduct is ethically appropriate.</p>\n<p>For example, management may avoid misleading customers even when a particular marketing practice is not explicitly prohibited by law.</p>\n<p>5. Administrative structure</p>\n<p>Administrative structure determines the organisation's responsibilities, reporting relationships and decision-making arrangements.</p>\n<p>A clear structure helps ensure that important issues are communicated to appropriate personnel and that risk-management duties are properly assigned.</p>\n<p>An unclear structure may cause risks to be overlooked because departments are uncertain about who is responsible.</p>\n<p>These elements are interconnected. Transparency is ineffective if management is not accountable for inaccurate disclosures, while accountability is difficult to enforce without clear responsibilities.</p>\n<p>Therefore, corporate governance should integrate all five elements rather than treating them as independent requirements.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Accountability + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transparency + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regulatory framework + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethics/social responsibility + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administrative structure + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>How to improve the answer further</h3>\n<p>If the question uses critically discuss, include an interaction among the five elements.</p>\n<p>Consider a hypothetical company where the finance director conceals project losses.</p>\n<p>The immediate problem involves:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Transparency</span></div>\n<p>If nobody holds the director responsible, there is also:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Accountability</span></div>\n<p>If the board never receives reliable information, the administrative and oversight arrangements may also be ineffective.</p>\n<p>The strongest answer recognises that a governance failure can involve several elements at once.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P3</span><span>Define risk governance. <small class=\"muted\">(12 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define risk governance. Discuss the seven key building blocks of risk governance and explain how they contribute to the implementation of effective ERM.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Risk governance refers to the structures and arrangements through which an organisation directs, oversees and establishes accountability for risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture explains that risk governance ensures risk-management and control frameworks are embedded in the organisation's culture, processes and structure.</p>\n<p>Seven important building blocks support its implementation.</p>\n<p>Corporate governance establishes top-down direction and oversight. The board and senior leadership define responsibilities and ensure that risk management supports corporate objectives.</p>\n<p>Line management connects risk management with the company's strategies and operational activities. Business managers remain responsible for managing the risks arising from their activities rather than transferring all responsibility to a central risk department.</p>\n<p>Portfolio management requires the organisation to assess risks collectively instead of only evaluating individual exposures. This helps identify concentrated exposures and potential interactions across different business activities.</p>\n<p>Risk transfer involves transferring selected exposures when this is economically appropriate. Insurance and suitable contractual arrangements may help reduce the financial burden of certain risks.</p>\n<p>Risk analytics supports informed decision-making through analytical tools used to assess, measure and monitor risks.</p>\n<p>Data and technology resources provide the information and systems required to support risk assessment, reporting and communication across the enterprise.</p>\n<p>Stakeholder management promotes transparency and appropriate communication of material risks to shareholders, regulators, employees and other relevant stakeholders.</p>\n<p>Together, these building blocks support a more integrated approach to risk management.</p>\n<p>For example, an organisation considering an overseas expansion may use risk analytics to evaluate exposure, portfolio management to understand combined risks, line management to implement controls and corporate governance to ensure appropriate oversight.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the building blocks collectively strengthen ERM by integrating risk-management responsibilities, information and decisions throughout the organisation.</p>\n<p>This follows the lecture's seven-part risk-governance model on page 9.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition and role of risk governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seven building blocks correctly explained</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application to enterprise risk management</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrated conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>One distinction that can earn extra marks</h3>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Risk Governance}\\neq\\text{Risk Management}}$$</div>\n<p>Risk governance establishes the authority, oversight and accountability arrangements.</p>\n<p>Risk management performs the identification, assessment, treatment and monitoring activities.</p>\n<p>A company may have a sophisticated risk assessment system but weak risk governance if directors ignore its findings.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P4</span><span>Critically analyse the relationship between corporate governance and risk management. <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Critically analyse the relationship between corporate governance and risk management. Explain why both are necessary to protect and create long-term corporate value.</p>\n<p>This is one of the most important questions in Topic 3 because it brings together Topics 1, 2 and 3.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Corporate governance and risk management are closely related because both support the achievement of organisational objectives and the protection of enterprise value.</p>\n<p>Corporate governance provides the structures, policies and accountability arrangements through which the company is directed and controlled.</p>\n<p>Risk management provides a systematic process for identifying, assessing, treating and monitoring uncertainty that may affect those objectives.</p>\n<p>The relationship is two-directional.</p>\n<p>First, corporate governance supports risk management by establishing clear responsibilities and oversight.</p>\n<p>For example, the board may require management to identify principal enterprise risks, implement appropriate controls and report material exposures regularly.</p>\n<p>Without effective governance, risk-management policies may exist formally but fail to influence actual decisions.</p>\n<p>Second, risk management supports corporate governance by providing decision-makers with information about the exposures associated with corporate strategy and operations.</p>\n<p>For example, before approving a major acquisition, directors require information about financial, strategic, operational and regulatory risks to make an informed decision.</p>\n<p>This allows governance bodies to challenge management assumptions and exercise oversight more effectively.</p>\n<p>Weaknesses in either system may damage corporate value.</p>\n<p>A company with strong governance structures but poor risk information may approve inappropriate decisions. Conversely, a company with excellent risk reports but ineffective board oversight may fail to act on identified risks.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective corporate governance and risk management must operate as complementary systems.</p>\n<p>Together, they promote informed decision-making, appropriate risk-taking, accountability and the achievement of long-term organisational objectives.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain corporate governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain risk management</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governance's contribution to RM</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM's contribution to governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation of their interaction</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The important analytical connection</h3>\n<p>Corporate governance</p>\n<p>Establishes authority, oversight, reporting and accountability</p>\n<p>Mutually reinforcing</p>\n<p>Risk management</p>\n<p>Supplies risk information, analysis, treatment and monitoring</p>\n<p>Better decisions, resilience and long-term value</p>\n<p>The diagrams on pages 12-17 of the lecture illustrate this two-way relationship and distinguish board oversight from executive implementation.</p>\n<p>A useful concluding sentence for the examination is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Corporate governance determines whether risk management is appropriately directed and accountable, while risk management provides the information needed for governance to operate effectively.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P5</span><span>Explain why the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 was introduced. <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain why the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 was introduced. Discuss its major objectives and evaluate how it strengthens corporate governance and risk management.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX) was enacted in the United States in 2002 following major corporate scandals, including those involving Enron and WorldCom.</p>\n<p>These scandals raised significant concerns about the reliability of corporate financial reporting, internal controls, executive accountability and investor protection.</p>\n<p>SOX was introduced to strengthen corporate accountability and restore confidence in financial markets.</p>\n<p>First, SOX seeks to improve the accuracy and reliability of corporate financial disclosures.</p>\n<p>Reliable financial reporting is essential because investors depend on corporate information when making investment decisions.</p>\n<p>Misleading or inaccurate financial statements may distort investment decisions and undermine confidence in capital markets.</p>\n<p>Second, SOX strengthens corporate controls and financial-reporting accountability.</p>\n<p>This encourages organisations to establish procedures that reduce opportunities for fraudulent reporting and material errors.</p>\n<p>For example, appropriate approval procedures, segregation of duties and independent review can reduce the likelihood of unauthorised transactions or inaccurate financial information.</p>\n<p>Third, SOX increases accountability for corporate executives and other relevant parties.</p>\n<p>The legislation strengthens responsibilities concerning the reliability of financial disclosures and introduces consequences for misconduct.</p>\n<p>Fourth, SOX seeks to deter corporate fraud and wrongdoing.</p>\n<p>Greater regulatory scrutiny and penalties can discourage deliberate manipulation of corporate information.</p>\n<p>From a risk-management perspective, SOX supports the control of financial-reporting and governance risks.</p>\n<p>However, it should not be regarded as a complete substitute for ERM.</p>\n<p>A company may maintain strong financial-reporting controls while still making unsuccessful strategic decisions or experiencing major operational disruptions.</p>\n<p>Therefore, SOX contributes to corporate governance by strengthening disclosure, internal control and accountability, while broader enterprise risks must still be managed through appropriate risk-management frameworks.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies SOX's origins, objectives and governance implications on pages 21-22.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Background: Enron, WorldCom and 2002 enactment</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial disclosure reliability</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal controls</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accountability and fraud deterrence</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation of benefits and limitations</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Advanced examiner trap</h3>\n<p>Do not write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>SOX eliminates the possibility of corporate fraud.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Controls can fail, management may override procedures, and individuals may collude.</p>\n<p>The stronger interpretation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"SOX → Stronger Reporting Controls → Greater Accountability → Improved Investor Protection\"><span>SOX</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Stronger Reporting Controls</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Greater Accountability</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Improved Investor Protection</span></div>\n<p>But:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Regulatory Compliance}\\neq\\text{Zero Enterprise Risk}}$$</div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P6</span><span>Discuss the Malaysian corporate governance and regulatory framework. <small class=\"muted\">(12 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Discuss the Malaysian corporate governance and regulatory framework. Explain the roles of the Securities Commission, Bursa Malaysia, Bank Negara Malaysia and the Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance in supporting effective risk management.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Malaysia's corporate governance framework consists of relevant legislation, regulatory requirements, governance codes and guidance intended to improve organisational accountability, risk management and stakeholder protection.</p>\n<p>These arrangements support the integration of risk management into corporate governance.</p>\n<p>The Securities Commission Malaysia (SC) plays an important role in promoting corporate governance practices within Malaysia's capital market.</p>\n<p>The Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance (MCCG), associated with the SC, provides principles and best practices for strengthening board effectiveness, governance arrangements and corporate accountability.</p>\n<p>From the perspective of ERM, the lecture highlights the importance of identifying principal risks and implementing appropriate systems to manage them.</p>\n<p>Bursa Malaysia provides listing-related governance requirements and guidance concerning risk management and internal control for public listed companies.</p>\n<p>The lecture refers to the Corporate Governance Guide - Towards Boardroom Excellence and the Statement on Risk Management and Internal Control guidance.</p>\n<p>These arrangements encourage boards and management to establish appropriate systems for identifying, managing and reporting material risks.</p>\n<p>Bank Negara Malaysia (BNM) provides governance and risk-governance requirements relevant to financial institutions.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies guidance associated with licensed institutions, insurers and broader risk governance.</p>\n<p>Such requirements are particularly important because failures in financial institutions may affect depositors, policyholders and the wider financial system.</p>\n<p>The Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance supports the adoption of good governance principles and practices.</p>\n<p>The lecture traces its development from the first code issued in 2000 through subsequent revisions, including the 2021 update.</p>\n<p>The later developments highlighted in the slides emphasise stronger board policies, director appointment processes, board oversight and the integration of sustainability considerations into strategy and operations.</p>\n<p>These institutions and frameworks have complementary functions.</p>\n<p>While governance codes provide principles and recommended practices, relevant laws, regulatory requirements and listing obligations establish enforceable expectations for organisations within their respective scope.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Malaysia's corporate governance and regulatory arrangements support ERM by encouraging accountability, appropriate risk oversight, internal controls and responsible corporate decision-making.</p>\n<p>This answer follows the Malaysian regulatory-framework discussion and institutional mapping on pages 23-27.</p>\n<h3>Important Malaysian regulatory mapping</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body</th>\n<th>Main association in the lecture</th>\n<th>Principal organisations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Securities Commission</td>\n<td>Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance</td>\n<td>Public listed companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bursa Malaysia</td>\n<td>Corporate governance and risk/internal-control guidance</td>\n<td>Listed issuers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bank Negara Malaysia</td>\n<td>Risk governance and prudential governance requirements</td>\n<td>Financial institutions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SSM</td>\n<td>Company and business legislative framework</td>\n<td>Registered companies/businesses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Putrajaya Committee on GLC High Performance</td>\n<td>Green Book - Enhancing Board Effectiveness</td>\n<td>Government-linked companies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>A distinction that can earn higher-level marks</h3>\n<p>The MCCG and Bursa's governance/disclosure requirements should not be treated as identical instruments.</p>\n<p>Your slides distinguish the governance code from Bursa's risk-management and internal-control guidance. The MCCG establishes governance practices, while relevant listing rules and regulatory obligations operate alongside it.</p>\n<p>This is also why the absence of a Malaysian law identical to SOX does not mean that Malaysian listed companies have no binding governance, financial-reporting or internal-control responsibilities.</p>\n<p>For examination purposes, a good answer should explain the relationship between the frameworks, rather than simply listing regulatory bodies.</p>\n<p>The lecture contains historical regulatory descriptions. This model answer follows its conceptual scope; precise present-day legal obligations and applicable editions would require a separate regulatory verification.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P7</span><span>Explain the three dimensions and eight components of the COSO ERM framework. <small class=\"muted\">(14 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the three dimensions and eight components of the COSO ERM framework. Evaluate how the framework can support effective enterprise-wide risk management.</p>\n<p>This is one of the most important framework questions to master.</p>\n<p>Your lecture presents the COSO 2004 ERM cube, organised into three dimensions and eight components.</p>\n<h5>Three dimensions</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{COSO ERM} = \\text{Objectives} \\times \\text{ERM Components} \\times \\text{Organisational Levels} }$$</div>\n<p>The dimensions describe what the organisation seeks to achieve, how risks are managed and where in the organisation the framework is applied.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The COSO Enterprise Risk Management framework provides a structured approach for identifying, assessing and managing risks in relation to organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>The COSO ERM model presented in the lecture consists of three interconnected dimensions.</p>\n<p>The first dimension is organisational objectives. These establish what the company seeks to achieve and provide the basis for identifying events that may affect the achievement of those objectives.</p>\n<p>The second dimension is ERM components. These represent the processes and activities used to identify, assess, respond to and monitor risks.</p>\n<p>The third dimension is organisational levels. This ensures that risk management applies across the enterprise, including business units, divisions and relevant organisational functions.</p>\n<p>The COSO 2004 framework presented in the lecture has eight components.</p>\n<p>1. Internal Environment</p>\n<p>This establishes the organisation's risk culture, ethical standards, management attitudes and internal governance environment.</p>\n<p>A weak internal environment may undermine risk management even when formal procedures exist.</p>\n<p>2. Objective Setting</p>\n<p>Management establishes objectives before identifying events that may affect their achievement.</p>\n<p>For example, an organisation seeking international expansion should define its growth and financial objectives before evaluating the associated risks.</p>\n<p>3. Event Identification</p>\n<p>Management identifies internal and external events that may affect organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>Examples include supplier disruption, changes in regulation, economic instability and technological failure.</p>\n<p>4. Risk Assessment</p>\n<p>Identified risks are assessed to understand their significance.</p>\n<p>This may involve evaluating the likelihood and consequences of potential events.</p>\n<p>5. Risk Response</p>\n<p>Management selects appropriate responses to identified risks.</p>\n<p>These responses may include avoiding, reducing, transferring or accepting particular exposures, depending on the circumstances.</p>\n<p>6. Control Activities</p>\n<p>Control activities consist of policies and procedures that help ensure management's risk responses are implemented.</p>\n<p>Examples include segregation of duties, transaction approvals and system-access controls.</p>\n<p>7. Information and Communication</p>\n<p>Relevant risk information must be collected and communicated to appropriate individuals and organisational bodies.</p>\n<p>For example, material project risks should be reported to management and the board to support informed decisions.</p>\n<p>8. Monitoring</p>\n<p>The organisation must review risk-management activities and controls to determine whether they continue to operate effectively as the business environment changes.</p>\n<p>These components are interconnected rather than independent.</p>\n<p>The COSO framework supports ERM by integrating risk-management activities into organisational objectives and decision-making across different levels of the enterprise.</p>\n<p>However, effective implementation depends on management commitment, reliable information, appropriate resources and continuous monitoring.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the value of COSO lies not merely in adopting its formal structure but in ensuring its components influence actual organisational decisions and behaviour.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain three dimensions</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eight components</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise-wide application</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation and conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>14</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>How to answer without memorising eight disconnected paragraphs</h3>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>Internal Environment</p>\n<p>What kind of organisation are we?</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>Objective Setting</p>\n<p>What do we want to achieve?</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>Event Identification</p>\n<p>What could happen?</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>Risk Assessment</p>\n<p>How significant is it?</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>Risk Response</p>\n<p>What should we do?</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>Control Activities</p>\n<p>How do we implement the response?</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>Information &amp; Communication</p>\n<p>Who needs to know?</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>Monitoring</p>\n<p>Is the system still working?</p>\n<p>The sequence is a revision aid for the eight components in the lecture's COSO 2004 model. The actual framework is an interconnected system, not a process that happens once and ends.</p>\n<h3>Higher-order insight</h3>\n<p>Suppose management establishes a revenue-growth target.</p>\n<p>The company then identifies a major supplier dependency, assesses its significance and decides to qualify alternative suppliers.</p>\n<p>That response requires controls and monitoring.</p>\n<p>Now imagine that the board never receives a report on supplier dependency.</p>\n<p>The company has not necessarily failed at event identification. It may have failed at information and communication, despite completing earlier risk-management activities.</p>\n<p>This shows why you must be able to identify which COSO component is missing, rather than describing the entire framework in every answer.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P8</span><span>Explain the major components and principles of ISO 31000. <small class=\"muted\">(12 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the major components and principles of ISO 31000. Evaluate how ISO 31000 helps organisations integrate risk management into their activities and decisions.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically describes ISO 31000:2009 and divides the standard into three parts: principles, framework and process.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>ISO 31000 is a risk-management standard developed by the International Organization for Standardization to provide guidance for managing risks across organisational activities.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes ISO 31000:2009 as consisting of three main components: principles and guidelines, a risk-management framework, and a risk-management process.</p>\n<p>The first component concerns principles and guidelines.</p>\n<p>The principles establish the characteristics of effective risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies eleven principles, including creating value, integrating risk management into organisational processes, supporting decision-making, addressing uncertainty, using the best available information, considering human and cultural factors, and promoting continual improvement.</p>\n<p>These principles help ensure that risk management is relevant to organisational objectives rather than merely an administrative activity.</p>\n<p>For example, the principle that risk management should be part of decision-making means that major investment risks should be assessed before management commits resources.</p>\n<p>The second component is the risk-management framework.</p>\n<p>The framework establishes the organisational structures and arrangements required to implement risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies five main framework components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mandate and commitment.</li>\n<li>Design of the framework.</li>\n<li>Implementation of risk management.</li>\n<li>Monitoring and review of the framework.</li>\n<li>Continual improvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management commitment is particularly important because a framework may fail if leadership does not provide adequate resources, authority or organisational support.</p>\n<p>The third component is the risk-management process.</p>\n<p>The process concerns the practical activities through which risks are identified, analysed, evaluated, treated, communicated and monitored in relation to organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturer may identify fire exposure, evaluate its probability and consequences, implement appropriate protection systems and periodically monitor the effectiveness of those controls.</p>\n<p>ISO 31000 is valuable because it encourages an integrated and adaptable approach to managing uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Unlike a rigid set of identical rules for every company, the lecture emphasises that risk management should be tailored to organisational circumstances and responsive to change.</p>\n<p>However, adopting ISO 31000 does not automatically guarantee that an organisation will avoid all losses.</p>\n<p>Its effectiveness depends on actual implementation, reliable risk information and continual management commitment.</p>\n<p>Therefore, ISO 31000 provides a structured basis for integrating risk management into organisational processes, improving decision-making and supporting long-term objectives.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition and purpose</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principles and their relevance</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Framework and five components</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-management process</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation/conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P9</span><span>Compare the COSO and ISO 31000 risk-management frameworks. <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Compare the COSO and ISO 31000 risk-management frameworks. Evaluate their similarities, differences and importance to organisations.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>COSO and ISO 31000 are recognised approaches that provide guidance for managing organisational risk.</p>\n<p>Both support systematic risk management, the achievement of organisational objectives and the integration of risk considerations into management decisions.</p>\n<p>The main similarity is that both recognise the importance of identifying, assessing, responding to and monitoring risks.</p>\n<p>They also encourage organisations to establish appropriate responsibilities and processes rather than relying on informal or purely reactive approaches.</p>\n<p>However, the two frameworks are presented differently in the lecture.</p>\n<p>COSO ERM is illustrated through a three-dimensional model encompassing organisational objectives, ERM components and organisational levels.</p>\n<p>Its eight components include internal environment, objective setting, event identification, risk assessment, risk response, control activities, information and communication, and monitoring.</p>\n<p>COSO therefore provides a structured way to consider how risk-management activities operate across an enterprise.</p>\n<p>ISO 31000, on the other hand, is presented through principles, a framework and a process.</p>\n<p>Its principles emphasise value creation, integration, decision-making, reliable information, adaptability and continual improvement.</p>\n<p>The ISO framework also emphasises organisational commitment, implementation, monitoring and improvement.</p>\n<p>The selection or application of these frameworks should reflect an organisation's needs, objectives and operational context.</p>\n<p>For example, an organisation seeking a structured enterprise-wide view of risk responsibilities and activities may find the COSO model useful.</p>\n<p>An organisation seeking broad principles and a tailored risk-management approach may use ISO 31000 guidance.</p>\n<p>However, these are not mutually exclusive alternatives, and organisations may draw on both.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the primary objective is not to adopt a framework merely for recognition but to establish an effective risk-management system that supports informed decisions and organisational value.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Similarities</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>COSO model and components</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO structure and principles</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluated comparison and conclusion</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>A very useful summary</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>COSO 2004 ERM</th>\n<th>ISO 31000:2009</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary lecture model</td>\n<td>Three-dimensional ERM cube</td>\n<td>Principles, framework and process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main structural emphasis</td>\n<td>Objectives, components, organisational levels</td>\n<td>Integration, organisational framework, process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Component focus</td>\n<td>Eight ERM components</td>\n<td>Eleven principles and five framework components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical application</td>\n<td>Structured enterprise-wide risk management</td>\n<td>Broad, tailored risk-management guidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared purpose</td>\n<td>Support organisational objectives</td>\n<td>Support organisational objectives</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These comparisons refer to the historical editions specifically shown in your lecture. They should not be presented as a comparison of the latest editions without verifying those editions separately.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P10</span><span>Briefly explain AS/NZS 4360, ISO/IEC 31010 and ISO Guide 73 and their roles in risk management. <small class=\"muted\">(6 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Briefly explain AS/NZS 4360, ISO/IEC 31010 and ISO Guide 73 and their roles in risk management.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>AS/NZS 4360 is a risk-management standard developed through cooperation between Australian and New Zealand standards organisations.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes it as providing generic guidance for establishing context, identifying risks, assessing and prioritising them, treating them, monitoring and reviewing them, and communicating with relevant stakeholders.</p>\n<p>ISO/IEC 31010 provides guidance on risk-assessment techniques. Its purpose is to support the use of appropriate analytical methods when assessing different types of risk.</p>\n<p>ISO Guide 73 provides a vocabulary of risk-management terms and definitions.</p>\n<p>These standards and guidance documents support risk management in complementary ways.</p>\n<p>AS/NZS 4360 provides a general process structure, ISO/IEC 31010 supports the selection of assessment techniques, and ISO Guide 73 promotes consistency in terminology.</p>\n<p>Together, they can improve the clarity, consistency and quality of organisational risk-management activities.</p>\n<p>These roles are outlined on pages 31-34.</p>\n<h3>Revision priority</h3>\n<p>Understand AS/NZS 4360 and the two ISO companion documents, but devote more detailed preparation to COSO and ISO 31000, because they are the two major ERM frameworks developed in depth in Topic 3.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P11</span><span>Explain the concept of Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC). <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the concept of Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC). Evaluate why integrating these functions is more effective than managing them independently.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC) refers to the coordinated management of an organisation's governance arrangements, risk-management activities and compliance obligations.</p>\n<p>Governance establishes the structures, responsibilities, oversight and accountability through which an organisation is directed and controlled.</p>\n<p>Risk management involves identifying, assessing, responding to and monitoring uncertainties affecting organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>Compliance concerns adherence to applicable laws, regulations, organisational policies and other relevant requirements.</p>\n<p>Although these functions have different responsibilities, they are interconnected.</p>\n<p>For example, a Malaysian listed company considering a major acquisition requires governance arrangements for board approval, risk management to assess financial and strategic exposures, and compliance procedures to satisfy applicable legal and regulatory obligations.</p>\n<p>If the three functions operate independently, important information may fail to reach relevant decision-makers, while responsibilities may become fragmented.</p>\n<p>An integrated GRC approach helps coordinate risk information, organisational responsibilities and compliance activities.</p>\n<p>However, effective integration should preserve appropriate independence where necessary, particularly for oversight and assurance functions.</p>\n<p>Therefore, GRC can strengthen organisational decision-making and accountability by ensuring that governance, risk management and compliance operate as complementary parts of the enterprise's management system.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking allocation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Governance explained</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk management explained</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compliance explained</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integration and evaluation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Examiner's trap</h3>\n<p>Do not confuse compliance with governance.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance asks: Are required rules being followed?</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance asks: Who directs, oversees and is accountable?</span></div>\n<p>Compliance may operate within a governance system, but the concepts are not identical.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P12</span><span>XYZ Berhad, a Malaysian public listed manufacturing company, has recently undertaken several major investment projects. <small class=\"muted\">(20 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>XYZ Berhad, a Malaysian public listed manufacturing company, has recently undertaken several major investment projects.</p>\n<p>Management aggressively pursues revenue growth, and executive bonuses are linked mainly to short-term financial performance.</p>\n<p>The company's internal auditors identify weaknesses in financial reporting and major purchasing controls. However, senior management does not report these findings to the board.</p>\n<p>The board receives optimistic financial forecasts but rarely challenges management assumptions.</p>\n<p>In addition, XYZ Berhad has a comprehensive written risk-management policy, but individual departments do not consistently implement it.</p>\n<p>Required:</p>\n<p>(a) Identify and analyse three major corporate governance weaknesses. (6 marks)</p>\n<p>(b) Explain how the COSO framework could address these weaknesses. (6 marks)</p>\n<p>(c) Recommend improvements using ISO 31000 principles and framework components. (5 marks)</p>\n<p>(d) Explain how the Malaysian corporate governance framework supports greater accountability. (3 marks)</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h2>Model answer - (a) Governance weaknesses (6 marks)</h2>\n<p>1. Weak transparency and information flow</p>\n<p>Management fails to communicate internal-audit findings to the board.</p>\n<p>This prevents directors from obtaining a complete understanding of the company's financial-reporting and purchasing-control weaknesses.</p>\n<p>Without reliable information, the board may be unable to exercise effective oversight.</p>\n<p>2. Inadequate accountability and board oversight</p>\n<p>The board does not sufficiently challenge management's financial forecasts or major investment assumptions.</p>\n<p>This may allow management to undertake excessive or poorly evaluated risks without appropriate scrutiny.</p>\n<p>3. Weak risk governance and organisational culture</p>\n<p>Although the company possesses a written risk-management policy, departments do not consistently implement it.</p>\n<p>This indicates that risk management is not adequately embedded within organisational processes and behaviour.</p>\n<p>The company's short-term bonus incentives may further encourage risk-taking that is inconsistent with long-term organisational interests.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - (b) Apply COSO (6 marks)</h2>\n<p>COSO could help the organisation strengthen risk management through several interconnected components.</p>\n<p>The internal environment should be improved by promoting accountability, ethical conduct and a culture in which internal-control weaknesses are taken seriously.</p>\n<p>Through objective setting, the board should establish corporate objectives that balance short-term growth with acceptable risk-taking and longer-term value.</p>\n<p>Event identification and risk assessment should be used to identify weaknesses in financial reporting, purchasing controls and investment decisions and assess their potential consequences.</p>\n<p>Risk response and control activities should address identified weaknesses through appropriate approval procedures, segregation of duties and independent reviews.</p>\n<p>Information and communication should ensure that internal-audit findings and material risks are communicated to relevant governance bodies.</p>\n<p>Finally, monitoring should evaluate whether controls remain effective and whether departments consistently implement the risk-management framework.</p>\n<p>These measures would help ensure that COSO operates as a management system rather than merely a formal policy.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - (c) Apply ISO 31000 (5 marks)</h2>\n<p>XYZ Berhad should improve its risk-management system using relevant ISO 31000 principles and framework components.</p>\n<p>First, the board should demonstrate mandate and commitment by formally supporting the risk-management framework and assigning appropriate responsibilities.</p>\n<p>Second, management should design and implement procedures that integrate risk assessments into purchasing, investment approval and financial-reporting processes.</p>\n<p>Third, the company should apply the ISO principle that risk management is part of decision-making, ensuring major investment proposals are evaluated before approval.</p>\n<p>Fourth, risk assessment should use the best available information, including reliable internal-audit findings and realistic financial assumptions.</p>\n<p>Fifth, the company should conduct regular monitoring, review and continual improvement, particularly where controls are found to be ineffective.</p>\n<p>These changes would make risk management more closely aligned with the organisation's actual behaviour and objectives.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - (d) Malaysian regulatory framework (3 marks)</h2>\n<p>The Malaysian corporate governance framework encourages listed companies to identify principal risks and establish appropriate systems for managing them.</p>\n<p>The Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance provides principles and practices supporting board oversight and accountability.</p>\n<p>Bursa Malaysia's governance and risk-management/internal-control requirements also provide relevant expectations for listed issuers.</p>\n<p>XYZ Berhad should therefore strengthen board oversight, ensure material risks and control weaknesses are appropriately communicated, and maintain reliable governance and risk-management processes consistent with applicable requirements.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P13</span><span>12 minutes A Malaysian public listed company has unreliable financial disclosures, unclear managerial...</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>12 minutes</p>\n<p>A Malaysian public listed company has unreliable financial disclosures, unclear managerial responsibilities and allegations of unethical supplier practices. Using the five elements of corporate governance, analyse the weaknesses and recommend improvements.</p>\n<p>Your exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer</p>\n<p>Suggested marking points</p>\n<p>Correct explanation of five governance elements</p>\n<p>Specific connection to each case issue</p>\n<p>Identification of accountability/transparency problems</p>\n<p>Explanation of regulatory and ethics implications</p>\n<p>Realistic corrective actions and justification</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P14</span><span>10 minutes Explain and critically analyse the two-way relationship between corporate governance and risk management.</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>10 minutes</p>\n<p>Explain and critically analyse the two-way relationship between corporate governance and risk management. Illustrate your answer using a company considering a major acquisition.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nAccurate explanation of governance and risk management</p>\n<p>How governance provides oversight and accountability</p>\n<p>How risk information supports board decisions</p>\n<p>Specific acquisition application</p>\n<p>Balanced evaluation</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P15</span><span>12 minutes Discuss the objectives of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002.</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>12 minutes</p>\n<p>Discuss the objectives of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002. Compare its role with the Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance and the Malaysian regulatory framework in strengthening corporate accountability.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nSOX historical motivation</p>\n<p>Financial disclosures, controls and accountability</p>\n<p>MCCG principles and role</p>\n<p>SC/Bursa Malaysia institutional context</p>\n<p>Meaningful comparison without treating laws and codes as identical</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P16</span><span>15 minutes A company has a weak risk culture, fails to identify major supplier dependencies, provides inaccurate...</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>15 minutes</p>\n<p>A company has a weak risk culture, fails to identify major supplier dependencies, provides inaccurate reports to its board and rarely reviews the effectiveness of controls. Explain the COSO ERM model and apply relevant components to address these weaknesses.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nThree dimensions of COSO</p>\n<p>Correct COSO 2004 component terminology</p>\n<p>Internal environment and event identification</p>\n<p>Information and communication</p>\n<p>Control activities and monitoring</p>\n<p>Evaluation of interconnected implementation</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P17</span><span>12 minutes A manufacturing company copies an unrelated bank's risk-management policy.</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>12 minutes</p>\n<p>A manufacturing company copies an unrelated bank's risk-management policy. Its senior executives provide little support, it uses outdated risk data and employees are excluded from risk discussions. Evaluate the weaknesses using ISO 31000:2009 principles and recommend improvements to its risk-management framework.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nISO principles, framework and process</p>\n<p>Tailored principle</p>\n<p>Best available information</p>\n<p>Human/cultural and inclusive principles</p>\n<p>Mandate/commitment, design, implementation and continual improvement</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P18</span><span>14 minutes A company publishes accurate annual financial statements but routinely approves high-risk strategic...</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>14 minutes</p>\n<p>A company publishes accurate annual financial statements but routinely approves high-risk strategic projects without independent review. Its board relies solely on management presentations and assumes compliance proves risk management is effective. Critically evaluate this claim and recommend an appropriate governance and ERM response.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nDifferentiate compliance and broader ERM</p>\n<p>Identify board oversight and information problems</p>\n<p>Explain strategic risk</p>\n<p>Apply COSO or ISO appropriately</p>\n<p>Justify governance improvements and acknowledge limitations</p></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l3\" id=\"t3s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">17 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Part 1 - How to answer subjective questions in Topic 3\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s1\" title=\"Part 1 - How to answer subjective questions in Topic 3\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P1</span>How to answer subjective questions in Topic 3</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - Corporate governance vs management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s2\" title=\"Part 2 - Corporate governance vs management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P2</span>Corporate governance vs management</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - The five elements of corporate governance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s3\" title=\"Part 3 - The five elements of corporate governance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P3</span>The five elements of corporate governance</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - Risk governance and its seven building blocks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s4\" title=\"Part 4 - Risk governance and its seven building blocks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P4</span>Risk governance and its seven building blocks</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - Relationship between corporate governance and risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s5\" title=\"Part 5 - Relationship between corporate governance and risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P5</span>Governance and risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s6\" title=\"Part 6 - Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P6</span>Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX)</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - Malaysian corporate governance and regulatory framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s7\" title=\"Part 7 - Malaysian corporate governance and regulatory framework\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P7</span>Malaysian framework</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - COSO Enterprise Risk Management Framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s8\" title=\"Part 8 - COSO Enterprise Risk Management Framework\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P8</span>COSO Enterprise Risk Management Framework</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - ISO 31000 Risk Management Standard\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s9\" title=\"Part 9 - ISO 31000 Risk Management Standard\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P9</span>ISO 31000 Risk Management Standard</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - COSO vs ISO 31000\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s10\" title=\"Part 10 - COSO vs ISO 31000\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P10</span>COSO vs ISO 31000</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - Other risk-management standards\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s11\" title=\"Part 11 - Other risk-management standards\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P11</span>Other risk-management standards</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s12\" title=\"Part 12 - Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P12</span>Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC)</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - Comprehensive 20-mark case study\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s13\" title=\"Part 13 - Comprehensive 20-mark case study\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P13</span>Comprehensive 20-mark case study</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - Common mistakes in Topic 3 subjective answers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s14\" title=\"Part 14 - Common mistakes in Topic 3 subjective answers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P14</span>Common mistakes in Topic 3 subjective answers</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 - Topic 3 subjective practice examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s15\" title=\"Part 15 - Topic 3 subjective practice examination\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P15</span>Topic 3 subjective practice examination</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 - What I would prioritise before the exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s16\" title=\"Part 16 - What I would prioritise before the exam\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P16</span>What I would prioritise before the exam</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 17 - Last-minute memory sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3s-s17\" title=\"Part 17 - Last-minute memory sheet\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P17</span>Last-minute memory sheet</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l3\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Topic 3 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>17 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>Enterprise Risk Management | Subjective Examination Preparation</p>\n<p>Topic 3 is particularly important for subjective examinations because it tests whether you understand how organisations turn risk-management principles into governance structures, accountability, controls and decision-making systems.</p>\n<p>Your lecture covers corporate governance, risk-management standards, SOX, COSO, ISO 31000 and the Malaysian regulatory framework.</p>\n<p>For this chapter, I would prepare you around six relationships:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Corporate Governance → Risk Governance → Risk Management → Internal Controls → Standards &amp; Regulation → Accountability\"><span>Corporate Governance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Governance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Management</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Internal Controls</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Standards &amp; Regulation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Accountability</span></div>\n<p>The questions below are original exam-style questions with suggested marks, not confirmed examination questions. I'll focus on applying, analysing, evaluating and designing solutions rather than merely defining terminology.</p><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - How to answer subjective questions in Topic 3</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Topic 2 asks why an organisation needs integrated risk management.</p>\n<p>Topic 3 asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Who is responsible for ensuring risk management is properly implemented, what standards guide it, and what mechanisms provide accountability?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For most 8-15 mark questions, use the following reasoning framework:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Define: Identify the relevant governance principle, framework or regulation.</li>\n<li>Explain: Describe its purpose and how it operates.</li>\n<li>Apply: Connect the principle to the business scenario.</li>\n<li>Analyse: Explain what could go wrong if the principle is not implemented.</li>\n<li>Evaluate: Recommend improvements and discuss their limitations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example, compare these two answers.</p>\n<p>Basic answer:</p>\n<p>\"Corporate governance is important because it reduces risk and improves company performance.\"</p>\n<p>Higher-order answer:</p>\n<p>\"Corporate governance establishes oversight, reporting and accountability mechanisms that guide management decisions. Without effective board oversight, management may undertake excessive or poorly evaluated risks, conceal material exposures or implement inadequate controls. This can contribute to financial losses and declining investor confidence. Therefore, effective governance must ensure that material risks are identified, communicated, challenged and managed in relation to corporate objectives.\"</p>\n<p>The second answer demonstrates why governance matters rather than simply stating that it matters.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - Corporate governance vs management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_gov_vs_mgmt\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Governance sits one level above execution: authority, oversight and accountability.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 1 - 8 marks</h3>\n<p>Define corporate governance and differentiate it from management. Explain why the distinction is important for effective organisational risk management.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Corporate governance refers to the structures, policies, laws and processes through which an organisation is directed, administered and controlled in the interests of shareholders and other stakeholders.</p>\n<p>Its primary objective is to enhance shareholder value while considering the interests of other stakeholders.</p>\n<p>Management is primarily responsible for conducting the organisation's daily activities, implementing strategies and allocating resources to achieve operational objectives.</p>\n<p>Corporate governance, on the other hand, establishes the overall system of direction, oversight, accountability and control within which management operates.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturing company's management may decide how to schedule production, purchase materials and maintain equipment.</p>\n<p>The board of directors, through its governance responsibilities, oversees the company's strategy, major risks and adequacy of relevant control systems.</p>\n<p>This distinction is important because management should not operate without appropriate oversight. Excessive concentration of decision-making authority without accountability may increase the likelihood of poor decisions, conflicts of interest or inadequate risk management.</p>\n<p>Conversely, governance that interferes excessively in routine operations may reduce management efficiency.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective corporate governance requires a clear separation of responsibilities, reliable reporting and sufficient oversight while allowing management to execute business activities efficiently.</p>\n<p>The lecture distinguishes daily management from governance and explains that weak governance can undermine operating performance and investor confidence.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition of corporate governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explanation of management</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clear distinction between the two</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevant application</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis of consequences and importance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>The deeper lesson</h4>\n<p>A company may have highly capable managers but still suffer serious governance failures.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Management Capability}\\neq\\text{Governance Quality}}$$</div>\n<p>Someone can be excellent at increasing revenue but poor at managing conflicts of interest, disclosing risks or respecting oversight.</p>\n<p>For a high-scoring answer, discuss the allocation of authority and accountability, not simply the organisational hierarchy.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - The five elements of corporate governance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Question 2 - 10 marks</h3>\n<p>Explain the five elements of corporate governance and critically discuss how weaknesses in these elements may expose an organisation to risk.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies accountability, transparency, regulatory framework, business ethics and social responsibility, and administrative structure as its five elements.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Effective corporate governance depends on several interconnected elements that promote responsible management, transparency and organisational accountability.</p>\n<p>1. Accountability</p>\n<p>Accountability ensures that individuals and organisational bodies are answerable for their decisions, responsibilities and performance.</p>\n<p>For example, senior management should be required to explain major investment decisions and their associated risks to the board.</p>\n<p>Without accountability, managers may undertake excessive risks or make decisions without facing appropriate scrutiny.</p>\n<p>2. Transparency</p>\n<p>Transparency refers to the appropriate disclosure and communication of relevant organisational information to stakeholders and decision-makers.</p>\n<p>For example, a listed company should provide reliable information concerning financial performance and material exposures.</p>\n<p>Weak transparency may prevent investors and directors from assessing the company's actual position, potentially reducing investor confidence.</p>\n<p>3. Regulatory framework</p>\n<p>The regulatory framework consists of the relevant laws, rules and formal requirements governing organisational activities.</p>\n<p>Compliance with these requirements helps establish minimum standards of acceptable corporate conduct.</p>\n<p>Failure to comply may result in regulatory action, legal liabilities and reputational damage.</p>\n<p>4. Business ethics and social responsibility</p>\n<p>This element encourages organisations to act responsibly towards employees, customers, shareholders and other stakeholders.</p>\n<p>It goes beyond simply complying with legal requirements by considering whether business conduct is ethically appropriate.</p>\n<p>For example, management may avoid misleading customers even when a particular marketing practice is not explicitly prohibited by law.</p>\n<p>5. Administrative structure</p>\n<p>Administrative structure determines the organisation's responsibilities, reporting relationships and decision-making arrangements.</p>\n<p>A clear structure helps ensure that important issues are communicated to appropriate personnel and that risk-management duties are properly assigned.</p>\n<p>An unclear structure may cause risks to be overlooked because departments are uncertain about who is responsible.</p>\n<p>These elements are interconnected. Transparency is ineffective if management is not accountable for inaccurate disclosures, while accountability is difficult to enforce without clear responsibilities.</p>\n<p>Therefore, corporate governance should integrate all five elements rather than treating them as independent requirements.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Accountability + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transparency + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regulatory framework + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethics/social responsibility + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administrative structure + explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>How to improve the answer further</h4>\n<p>If the question uses critically discuss, include an interaction among the five elements.</p>\n<p>Consider a hypothetical company where the finance director conceals project losses.</p>\n<p>The immediate problem involves:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Transparency</span></div>\n<p>If nobody holds the director responsible, there is also:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Accountability</span></div>\n<p>If the board never receives reliable information, the administrative and oversight arrangements may also be ineffective.</p>\n<p>The strongest answer recognises that a governance failure can involve several elements at once.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - Risk governance and its seven building blocks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Question 3 - 12 marks</h3>\n<p>Define risk governance. Discuss the seven key building blocks of risk governance and explain how they contribute to the implementation of effective ERM.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Risk governance refers to the structures and arrangements through which an organisation directs, oversees and establishes accountability for risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture explains that risk governance ensures risk-management and control frameworks are embedded in the organisation's culture, processes and structure.</p>\n<p>Seven important building blocks support its implementation.</p>\n<p>Corporate governance establishes top-down direction and oversight. The board and senior leadership define responsibilities and ensure that risk management supports corporate objectives.</p>\n<p>Line management connects risk management with the company's strategies and operational activities. Business managers remain responsible for managing the risks arising from their activities rather than transferring all responsibility to a central risk department.</p>\n<p>Portfolio management requires the organisation to assess risks collectively instead of only evaluating individual exposures. This helps identify concentrated exposures and potential interactions across different business activities.</p>\n<p>Risk transfer involves transferring selected exposures when this is economically appropriate. Insurance and suitable contractual arrangements may help reduce the financial burden of certain risks.</p>\n<p>Risk analytics supports informed decision-making through analytical tools used to assess, measure and monitor risks.</p>\n<p>Data and technology resources provide the information and systems required to support risk assessment, reporting and communication across the enterprise.</p>\n<p>Stakeholder management promotes transparency and appropriate communication of material risks to shareholders, regulators, employees and other relevant stakeholders.</p>\n<p>Together, these building blocks support a more integrated approach to risk management.</p>\n<p>For example, an organisation considering an overseas expansion may use risk analytics to evaluate exposure, portfolio management to understand combined risks, line management to implement controls and corporate governance to ensure appropriate oversight.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the building blocks collectively strengthen ERM by integrating risk-management responsibilities, information and decisions throughout the organisation.</p>\n<p>This follows the lecture's seven-part risk-governance model on page 9.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition and role of risk governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seven building blocks correctly explained</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application to enterprise risk management</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrated conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>One distinction that can earn extra marks</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Risk Governance}\\neq\\text{Risk Management}}$$</div>\n<p>Risk governance establishes the authority, oversight and accountability arrangements.</p>\n<p>Risk management performs the identification, assessment, treatment and monitoring activities.</p>\n<p>A company may have a sophisticated risk assessment system but weak risk governance if directors ignore its findings.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - Relationship between corporate governance and risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Question 4 - 10 marks</h3>\n<p>Critically analyse the relationship between corporate governance and risk management. Explain why both are necessary to protect and create long-term corporate value.</p>\n<p>This is one of the most important questions in Topic 3 because it brings together Topics 1, 2 and 3.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Corporate governance and risk management are closely related because both support the achievement of organisational objectives and the protection of enterprise value.</p>\n<p>Corporate governance provides the structures, policies and accountability arrangements through which the company is directed and controlled.</p>\n<p>Risk management provides a systematic process for identifying, assessing, treating and monitoring uncertainty that may affect those objectives.</p>\n<p>The relationship is two-directional.</p>\n<p>First, corporate governance supports risk management by establishing clear responsibilities and oversight.</p>\n<p>For example, the board may require management to identify principal enterprise risks, implement appropriate controls and report material exposures regularly.</p>\n<p>Without effective governance, risk-management policies may exist formally but fail to influence actual decisions.</p>\n<p>Second, risk management supports corporate governance by providing decision-makers with information about the exposures associated with corporate strategy and operations.</p>\n<p>For example, before approving a major acquisition, directors require information about financial, strategic, operational and regulatory risks to make an informed decision.</p>\n<p>This allows governance bodies to challenge management assumptions and exercise oversight more effectively.</p>\n<p>Weaknesses in either system may damage corporate value.</p>\n<p>A company with strong governance structures but poor risk information may approve inappropriate decisions. Conversely, a company with excellent risk reports but ineffective board oversight may fail to act on identified risks.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective corporate governance and risk management must operate as complementary systems.</p>\n<p>Together, they promote informed decision-making, appropriate risk-taking, accountability and the achievement of long-term organisational objectives.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain corporate governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain risk management</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governance's contribution to RM</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM's contribution to governance</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation of their interaction</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>The important analytical connection</h4>\n<p>Corporate governance</p>\n<p>Establishes authority, oversight, reporting and accountability</p>\n<p>Mutually reinforcing</p>\n<p>Risk management</p>\n<p>Supplies risk information, analysis, treatment and monitoring</p>\n<p>Better decisions, resilience and long-term value</p>\n<p>The diagrams on pages 12-17 of the lecture illustrate this two-way relationship and distinguish board oversight from executive implementation.</p>\n<p>A useful concluding sentence for the examination is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Corporate governance determines whether risk management is appropriately directed and accountable, while risk management provides the information needed for governance to operate effectively.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Question 5 - 10 marks</h3>\n<p>Explain why the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 was introduced. Discuss its major objectives and evaluate how it strengthens corporate governance and risk management.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>The Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX) was enacted in the United States in 2002 following major corporate scandals, including those involving Enron and WorldCom.</p>\n<p>These scandals raised significant concerns about the reliability of corporate financial reporting, internal controls, executive accountability and investor protection.</p>\n<p>SOX was introduced to strengthen corporate accountability and restore confidence in financial markets.</p>\n<p>First, SOX seeks to improve the accuracy and reliability of corporate financial disclosures.</p>\n<p>Reliable financial reporting is essential because investors depend on corporate information when making investment decisions.</p>\n<p>Misleading or inaccurate financial statements may distort investment decisions and undermine confidence in capital markets.</p>\n<p>Second, SOX strengthens corporate controls and financial-reporting accountability.</p>\n<p>This encourages organisations to establish procedures that reduce opportunities for fraudulent reporting and material errors.</p>\n<p>For example, appropriate approval procedures, segregation of duties and independent review can reduce the likelihood of unauthorised transactions or inaccurate financial information.</p>\n<p>Third, SOX increases accountability for corporate executives and other relevant parties.</p>\n<p>The legislation strengthens responsibilities concerning the reliability of financial disclosures and introduces consequences for misconduct.</p>\n<p>Fourth, SOX seeks to deter corporate fraud and wrongdoing.</p>\n<p>Greater regulatory scrutiny and penalties can discourage deliberate manipulation of corporate information.</p>\n<p>From a risk-management perspective, SOX supports the control of financial-reporting and governance risks.</p>\n<p>However, it should not be regarded as a complete substitute for ERM.</p>\n<p>A company may maintain strong financial-reporting controls while still making unsuccessful strategic decisions or experiencing major operational disruptions.</p>\n<p>Therefore, SOX contributes to corporate governance by strengthening disclosure, internal control and accountability, while broader enterprise risks must still be managed through appropriate risk-management frameworks.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies SOX's origins, objectives and governance implications on pages 21-22.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Background: Enron, WorldCom and 2002 enactment</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial disclosure reliability</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal controls</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accountability and fraud deterrence</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation of benefits and limitations</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Advanced examiner trap</h4>\n<p>Do not write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>SOX eliminates the possibility of corporate fraud.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Controls can fail, management may override procedures, and individuals may collude.</p>\n<p>The stronger interpretation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"SOX → Stronger Reporting Controls → Greater Accountability → Improved Investor Protection\"><span>SOX</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Stronger Reporting Controls</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Greater Accountability</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Improved Investor Protection</span></div>\n<p>But:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Regulatory Compliance}\\neq\\text{Zero Enterprise Risk}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - Malaysian corporate governance and regulatory framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Question 6 - 12 marks</h3>\n<p>Discuss the Malaysian corporate governance and regulatory framework. Explain the roles of the Securities Commission, Bursa Malaysia, Bank Negara Malaysia and the Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance in supporting effective risk management.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Malaysia's corporate governance framework consists of relevant legislation, regulatory requirements, governance codes and guidance intended to improve organisational accountability, risk management and stakeholder protection.</p>\n<p>These arrangements support the integration of risk management into corporate governance.</p>\n<p>The Securities Commission Malaysia (SC) plays an important role in promoting corporate governance practices within Malaysia's capital market.</p>\n<p>The Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance (MCCG), associated with the SC, provides principles and best practices for strengthening board effectiveness, governance arrangements and corporate accountability.</p>\n<p>From the perspective of ERM, the lecture highlights the importance of identifying principal risks and implementing appropriate systems to manage them.</p>\n<p>Bursa Malaysia provides listing-related governance requirements and guidance concerning risk management and internal control for public listed companies.</p>\n<p>The lecture refers to the Corporate Governance Guide - Towards Boardroom Excellence and the Statement on Risk Management and Internal Control guidance.</p>\n<p>These arrangements encourage boards and management to establish appropriate systems for identifying, managing and reporting material risks.</p>\n<p>Bank Negara Malaysia (BNM) provides governance and risk-governance requirements relevant to financial institutions.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies guidance associated with licensed institutions, insurers and broader risk governance.</p>\n<p>Such requirements are particularly important because failures in financial institutions may affect depositors, policyholders and the wider financial system.</p>\n<p>The Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance supports the adoption of good governance principles and practices.</p>\n<p>The lecture traces its development from the first code issued in 2000 through subsequent revisions, including the 2021 update.</p>\n<p>The later developments highlighted in the slides emphasise stronger board policies, director appointment processes, board oversight and the integration of sustainability considerations into strategy and operations.</p>\n<p>These institutions and frameworks have complementary functions.</p>\n<p>While governance codes provide principles and recommended practices, relevant laws, regulatory requirements and listing obligations establish enforceable expectations for organisations within their respective scope.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Malaysia's corporate governance and regulatory arrangements support ERM by encouraging accountability, appropriate risk oversight, internal controls and responsible corporate decision-making.</p>\n<p>This answer follows the Malaysian regulatory-framework discussion and institutional mapping on pages 23-27.</p>\n<h4>Important Malaysian regulatory mapping</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body</th>\n<th>Main association in the lecture</th>\n<th>Principal organisations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Securities Commission</td>\n<td>Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance</td>\n<td>Public listed companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bursa Malaysia</td>\n<td>Corporate governance and risk/internal-control guidance</td>\n<td>Listed issuers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bank Negara Malaysia</td>\n<td>Risk governance and prudential governance requirements</td>\n<td>Financial institutions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SSM</td>\n<td>Company and business legislative framework</td>\n<td>Registered companies/businesses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Putrajaya Committee on GLC High Performance</td>\n<td>Green Book - Enhancing Board Effectiveness</td>\n<td>Government-linked companies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>A distinction that can earn higher-level marks</h4>\n<p>The MCCG and Bursa's governance/disclosure requirements should not be treated as identical instruments.</p>\n<p>Your slides distinguish the governance code from Bursa's risk-management and internal-control guidance. The MCCG establishes governance practices, while relevant listing rules and regulatory obligations operate alongside it.</p>\n<p>This is also why the absence of a Malaysian law identical to SOX does not mean that Malaysian listed companies have no binding governance, financial-reporting or internal-control responsibilities.</p>\n<p>For examination purposes, a good answer should explain the relationship between the frameworks, rather than simply listing regulatory bodies.</p>\n<p>The lecture contains historical regulatory descriptions. This model answer follows its conceptual scope; precise present-day legal obligations and applicable editions would require a separate regulatory verification.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - COSO Enterprise Risk Management Framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t3_coso_cube3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">The COSO ERM cube in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · Pick an objective, a component and a level; the matching cell of the COSO cube lights up with an example.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 7 - 14 marks</h3>\n<p>Explain the three dimensions and eight components of the COSO ERM framework. Evaluate how the framework can support effective enterprise-wide risk management.</p>\n<p>This is one of the most important framework questions to master.</p>\n<p>Your lecture presents the COSO 2004 ERM cube, organised into three dimensions and eight components.</p>\n<h4>Three dimensions</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{COSO ERM} = \\text{Objectives} \\times \\text{ERM Components} \\times \\text{Organisational Levels} }$$</div>\n<p>The dimensions describe what the organisation seeks to achieve, how risks are managed and where in the organisation the framework is applied.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>The COSO Enterprise Risk Management framework provides a structured approach for identifying, assessing and managing risks in relation to organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>The COSO ERM model presented in the lecture consists of three interconnected dimensions.</p>\n<p>The first dimension is organisational objectives. These establish what the company seeks to achieve and provide the basis for identifying events that may affect the achievement of those objectives.</p>\n<p>The second dimension is ERM components. These represent the processes and activities used to identify, assess, respond to and monitor risks.</p>\n<p>The third dimension is organisational levels. This ensures that risk management applies across the enterprise, including business units, divisions and relevant organisational functions.</p>\n<p>The COSO 2004 framework presented in the lecture has eight components.</p>\n<p>1. Internal Environment</p>\n<p>This establishes the organisation's risk culture, ethical standards, management attitudes and internal governance environment.</p>\n<p>A weak internal environment may undermine risk management even when formal procedures exist.</p>\n<p>2. Objective Setting</p>\n<p>Management establishes objectives before identifying events that may affect their achievement.</p>\n<p>For example, an organisation seeking international expansion should define its growth and financial objectives before evaluating the associated risks.</p>\n<p>3. Event Identification</p>\n<p>Management identifies internal and external events that may affect organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>Examples include supplier disruption, changes in regulation, economic instability and technological failure.</p>\n<p>4. Risk Assessment</p>\n<p>Identified risks are assessed to understand their significance.</p>\n<p>This may involve evaluating the likelihood and consequences of potential events.</p>\n<p>5. Risk Response</p>\n<p>Management selects appropriate responses to identified risks.</p>\n<p>These responses may include avoiding, reducing, transferring or accepting particular exposures, depending on the circumstances.</p>\n<p>6. Control Activities</p>\n<p>Control activities consist of policies and procedures that help ensure management's risk responses are implemented.</p>\n<p>Examples include segregation of duties, transaction approvals and system-access controls.</p>\n<p>7. Information and Communication</p>\n<p>Relevant risk information must be collected and communicated to appropriate individuals and organisational bodies.</p>\n<p>For example, material project risks should be reported to management and the board to support informed decisions.</p>\n<p>8. Monitoring</p>\n<p>The organisation must review risk-management activities and controls to determine whether they continue to operate effectively as the business environment changes.</p>\n<p>These components are interconnected rather than independent.</p>\n<p>The COSO framework supports ERM by integrating risk-management activities into organisational objectives and decision-making across different levels of the enterprise.</p>\n<p>However, effective implementation depends on management commitment, reliable information, appropriate resources and continuous monitoring.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the value of COSO lies not merely in adopting its formal structure but in ensuring its components influence actual organisational decisions and behaviour.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain three dimensions</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eight components</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise-wide application</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation and conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>14</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>How to answer without memorising eight disconnected paragraphs</h4>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>Internal Environment</p>\n<p>What kind of organisation are we?</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>Objective Setting</p>\n<p>What do we want to achieve?</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>Event Identification</p>\n<p>What could happen?</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>Risk Assessment</p>\n<p>How significant is it?</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>Risk Response</p>\n<p>What should we do?</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>Control Activities</p>\n<p>How do we implement the response?</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>Information &amp; Communication</p>\n<p>Who needs to know?</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>Monitoring</p>\n<p>Is the system still working?</p>\n<p>The sequence is a revision aid for the eight components in the lecture's COSO 2004 model. The actual framework is an interconnected system, not a process that happens once and ends.</p>\n<h4>Higher-order insight</h4>\n<p>Suppose management establishes a revenue-growth target.</p>\n<p>The company then identifies a major supplier dependency, assesses its significance and decides to qualify alternative suppliers.</p>\n<p>That response requires controls and monitoring.</p>\n<p>Now imagine that the board never receives a report on supplier dependency.</p>\n<p>The company has not necessarily failed at event identification. It may have failed at information and communication, despite completing earlier risk-management activities.</p>\n<p>This shows why you must be able to identify which COSO component is missing, rather than describing the entire framework in every answer.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - ISO 31000 Risk Management Standard</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_iso_parts\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>ISO 31000 is three things: principles, a framework that organises RM, and a process.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 8 - 12 marks</h3>\n<p>Explain the major components and principles of ISO 31000. Evaluate how ISO 31000 helps organisations integrate risk management into their activities and decisions.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically describes ISO 31000:2009 and divides the standard into three parts: principles, framework and process.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>ISO 31000 is a risk-management standard developed by the International Organization for Standardization to provide guidance for managing risks across organisational activities.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes ISO 31000:2009 as consisting of three main components: principles and guidelines, a risk-management framework, and a risk-management process.</p>\n<p>The first component concerns principles and guidelines.</p>\n<p>The principles establish the characteristics of effective risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies eleven principles, including creating value, integrating risk management into organisational processes, supporting decision-making, addressing uncertainty, using the best available information, considering human and cultural factors, and promoting continual improvement.</p>\n<p>These principles help ensure that risk management is relevant to organisational objectives rather than merely an administrative activity.</p>\n<p>For example, the principle that risk management should be part of decision-making means that major investment risks should be assessed before management commits resources.</p>\n<p>The second component is the risk-management framework.</p>\n<p>The framework establishes the organisational structures and arrangements required to implement risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies five main framework components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mandate and commitment.</li>\n<li>Design of the framework.</li>\n<li>Implementation of risk management.</li>\n<li>Monitoring and review of the framework.</li>\n<li>Continual improvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management commitment is particularly important because a framework may fail if leadership does not provide adequate resources, authority or organisational support.</p>\n<p>The third component is the risk-management process.</p>\n<p>The process concerns the practical activities through which risks are identified, analysed, evaluated, treated, communicated and monitored in relation to organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturer may identify fire exposure, evaluate its probability and consequences, implement appropriate protection systems and periodically monitor the effectiveness of those controls.</p>\n<p>ISO 31000 is valuable because it encourages an integrated and adaptable approach to managing uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Unlike a rigid set of identical rules for every company, the lecture emphasises that risk management should be tailored to organisational circumstances and responsive to change.</p>\n<p>However, adopting ISO 31000 does not automatically guarantee that an organisation will avoid all losses.</p>\n<p>Its effectiveness depends on actual implementation, reliable risk information and continual management commitment.</p>\n<p>Therefore, ISO 31000 provides a structured basis for integrating risk management into organisational processes, improving decision-making and supporting long-term objectives.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition and purpose</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principles and their relevance</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Framework and five components</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk-management process</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation/conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The eleven principles: what to remember</h3>\n<p>Your lecture explicitly identifies the following eleven principles for the 2009 edition.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>Meaning in a subjective answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Creates value</td>\n<td>Improves decisions and supports objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integral to organisational processes</td>\n<td>Not isolated from normal business activities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Part of decision-making</td>\n<td>Risk information informs decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Addresses uncertainty</td>\n<td>Explicitly considers uncertain outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Systematic, structured and timely</td>\n<td>Uses a consistent and organised approach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best available information</td>\n<td>Uses relevant evidence while recognising limitations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tailored</td>\n<td>Fits organisational circumstances</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Human and cultural factors</td>\n<td>Considers behaviour, incentives and culture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transparent and inclusive</td>\n<td>Involves appropriate stakeholders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dynamic and responsive</td>\n<td>Adapts to changing conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continual improvement</td>\n<td>Regularly improves the risk-management system</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>The three principles I would practise applying most</h4>\n<p>Part of decision-making: A company should assess investment risk before approving a major expansion, not only after the decision.</p>\n<p>Tailored: A commercial bank and a manufacturing company should not blindly use identical risk-management controls because their activities and risk profiles differ.</p>\n<p>Dynamic and responsive: A company should update its framework as regulations, technologies and market conditions change.</p>\n<p>These examples are more valuable than memorising the principle names alone.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - COSO vs ISO 31000</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_framework_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Law, framework, standard and code: different nature, overlapping purpose.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 9 - 10 marks</h3>\n<p>Compare the COSO and ISO 31000 risk-management frameworks. Evaluate their similarities, differences and importance to organisations.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>COSO and ISO 31000 are recognised approaches that provide guidance for managing organisational risk.</p>\n<p>Both support systematic risk management, the achievement of organisational objectives and the integration of risk considerations into management decisions.</p>\n<p>The main similarity is that both recognise the importance of identifying, assessing, responding to and monitoring risks.</p>\n<p>They also encourage organisations to establish appropriate responsibilities and processes rather than relying on informal or purely reactive approaches.</p>\n<p>However, the two frameworks are presented differently in the lecture.</p>\n<p>COSO ERM is illustrated through a three-dimensional model encompassing organisational objectives, ERM components and organisational levels.</p>\n<p>Its eight components include internal environment, objective setting, event identification, risk assessment, risk response, control activities, information and communication, and monitoring.</p>\n<p>COSO therefore provides a structured way to consider how risk-management activities operate across an enterprise.</p>\n<p>ISO 31000, on the other hand, is presented through principles, a framework and a process.</p>\n<p>Its principles emphasise value creation, integration, decision-making, reliable information, adaptability and continual improvement.</p>\n<p>The ISO framework also emphasises organisational commitment, implementation, monitoring and improvement.</p>\n<p>The selection or application of these frameworks should reflect an organisation's needs, objectives and operational context.</p>\n<p>For example, an organisation seeking a structured enterprise-wide view of risk responsibilities and activities may find the COSO model useful.</p>\n<p>An organisation seeking broad principles and a tailored risk-management approach may use ISO 31000 guidance.</p>\n<p>However, these are not mutually exclusive alternatives, and organisations may draw on both.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the primary objective is not to adopt a framework merely for recognition but to establish an effective risk-management system that supports informed decisions and organisational value.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Similarities</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>COSO model and components</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO structure and principles</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluated comparison and conclusion</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>A very useful summary</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>COSO 2004 ERM</th>\n<th>ISO 31000:2009</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary lecture model</td>\n<td>Three-dimensional ERM cube</td>\n<td>Principles, framework and process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main structural emphasis</td>\n<td>Objectives, components, organisational levels</td>\n<td>Integration, organisational framework, process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Component focus</td>\n<td>Eight ERM components</td>\n<td>Eleven principles and five framework components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical application</td>\n<td>Structured enterprise-wide risk management</td>\n<td>Broad, tailored risk-management guidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared purpose</td>\n<td>Support organisational objectives</td>\n<td>Support organisational objectives</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These comparisons refer to the historical editions specifically shown in your lecture. They should not be presented as a comparison of the latest editions without verifying those editions separately.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - Other risk-management standards</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Question 10 - 6 marks</h3>\n<p>Briefly explain AS/NZS 4360, ISO/IEC 31010 and ISO Guide 73 and their roles in risk management.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>AS/NZS 4360 is a risk-management standard developed through cooperation between Australian and New Zealand standards organisations.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes it as providing generic guidance for establishing context, identifying risks, assessing and prioritising them, treating them, monitoring and reviewing them, and communicating with relevant stakeholders.</p>\n<p>ISO/IEC 31010 provides guidance on risk-assessment techniques. Its purpose is to support the use of appropriate analytical methods when assessing different types of risk.</p>\n<p>ISO Guide 73 provides a vocabulary of risk-management terms and definitions.</p>\n<p>These standards and guidance documents support risk management in complementary ways.</p>\n<p>AS/NZS 4360 provides a general process structure, ISO/IEC 31010 supports the selection of assessment techniques, and ISO Guide 73 promotes consistency in terminology.</p>\n<p>Together, they can improve the clarity, consistency and quality of organisational risk-management activities.</p>\n<p>These roles are outlined on pages 31-34.</p>\n<h4>Revision priority</h4>\n<p>Understand AS/NZS 4360 and the two ISO companion documents, but devote more detailed preparation to COSO and ISO 31000, because they are the two major ERM frameworks developed in depth in Topic 3.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_grc\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>GRC treats governance, risk and compliance as overlapping parts of one system, not three silos.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 11 - 8 marks</h3>\n<p>Explain the concept of Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC). Evaluate why integrating these functions is more effective than managing them independently.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC) refers to the coordinated management of an organisation's governance arrangements, risk-management activities and compliance obligations.</p>\n<p>Governance establishes the structures, responsibilities, oversight and accountability through which an organisation is directed and controlled.</p>\n<p>Risk management involves identifying, assessing, responding to and monitoring uncertainties affecting organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>Compliance concerns adherence to applicable laws, regulations, organisational policies and other relevant requirements.</p>\n<p>Although these functions have different responsibilities, they are interconnected.</p>\n<p>For example, a Malaysian listed company considering a major acquisition requires governance arrangements for board approval, risk management to assess financial and strategic exposures, and compliance procedures to satisfy applicable legal and regulatory obligations.</p>\n<p>If the three functions operate independently, important information may fail to reach relevant decision-makers, while responsibilities may become fragmented.</p>\n<p>An integrated GRC approach helps coordinate risk information, organisational responsibilities and compliance activities.</p>\n<p>However, effective integration should preserve appropriate independence where necessary, particularly for oversight and assurance functions.</p>\n<p>Therefore, GRC can strengthen organisational decision-making and accountability by ensuring that governance, risk management and compliance operate as complementary parts of the enterprise's management system.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Governance explained</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk management explained</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compliance explained</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integration and evaluation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Examiner's trap</h4>\n<p>Do not confuse compliance with governance.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance asks: Are required rules being followed?</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance asks: Who directs, oversees and is accountable?</span></div>\n<p>Compliance may operate within a governance system, but the concepts are not identical.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - Comprehensive 20-mark case study</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This case integrates the major Topic 3 concepts.</p>\n<h3>Question 12 - 20 marks</h3>\n<p>XYZ Berhad, a Malaysian public listed manufacturing company, has recently undertaken several major investment projects.</p>\n<p>Management aggressively pursues revenue growth, and executive bonuses are linked mainly to short-term financial performance.</p>\n<p>The company's internal auditors identify weaknesses in financial reporting and major purchasing controls. However, senior management does not report these findings to the board.</p>\n<p>The board receives optimistic financial forecasts but rarely challenges management assumptions.</p>\n<p>In addition, XYZ Berhad has a comprehensive written risk-management policy, but individual departments do not consistently implement it.</p>\n<p>Required:</p>\n<p>(a) Identify and analyse three major corporate governance weaknesses. (6 marks)</p>\n<p>(b) Explain how the COSO framework could address these weaknesses. (6 marks)</p>\n<p>(c) Recommend improvements using ISO 31000 principles and framework components. (5 marks)</p>\n<p>(d) Explain how the Malaysian corporate governance framework supports greater accountability. (3 marks)</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (a) Governance weaknesses (6 marks)</h3>\n<p>1. Weak transparency and information flow</p>\n<p>Management fails to communicate internal-audit findings to the board.</p>\n<p>This prevents directors from obtaining a complete understanding of the company's financial-reporting and purchasing-control weaknesses.</p>\n<p>Without reliable information, the board may be unable to exercise effective oversight.</p>\n<p>2. Inadequate accountability and board oversight</p>\n<p>The board does not sufficiently challenge management's financial forecasts or major investment assumptions.</p>\n<p>This may allow management to undertake excessive or poorly evaluated risks without appropriate scrutiny.</p>\n<p>3. Weak risk governance and organisational culture</p>\n<p>Although the company possesses a written risk-management policy, departments do not consistently implement it.</p>\n<p>This indicates that risk management is not adequately embedded within organisational processes and behaviour.</p>\n<p>The company's short-term bonus incentives may further encourage risk-taking that is inconsistent with long-term organisational interests.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (b) Apply COSO (6 marks)</h3>\n<p>COSO could help the organisation strengthen risk management through several interconnected components.</p>\n<p>The internal environment should be improved by promoting accountability, ethical conduct and a culture in which internal-control weaknesses are taken seriously.</p>\n<p>Through objective setting, the board should establish corporate objectives that balance short-term growth with acceptable risk-taking and longer-term value.</p>\n<p>Event identification and risk assessment should be used to identify weaknesses in financial reporting, purchasing controls and investment decisions and assess their potential consequences.</p>\n<p>Risk response and control activities should address identified weaknesses through appropriate approval procedures, segregation of duties and independent reviews.</p>\n<p>Information and communication should ensure that internal-audit findings and material risks are communicated to relevant governance bodies.</p>\n<p>Finally, monitoring should evaluate whether controls remain effective and whether departments consistently implement the risk-management framework.</p>\n<p>These measures would help ensure that COSO operates as a management system rather than merely a formal policy.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (c) Apply ISO 31000 (5 marks)</h3>\n<p>XYZ Berhad should improve its risk-management system using relevant ISO 31000 principles and framework components.</p>\n<p>First, the board should demonstrate mandate and commitment by formally supporting the risk-management framework and assigning appropriate responsibilities.</p>\n<p>Second, management should design and implement procedures that integrate risk assessments into purchasing, investment approval and financial-reporting processes.</p>\n<p>Third, the company should apply the ISO principle that risk management is part of decision-making, ensuring major investment proposals are evaluated before approval.</p>\n<p>Fourth, risk assessment should use the best available information, including reliable internal-audit findings and realistic financial assumptions.</p>\n<p>Fifth, the company should conduct regular monitoring, review and continual improvement, particularly where controls are found to be ineffective.</p>\n<p>These changes would make risk management more closely aligned with the organisation's actual behaviour and objectives.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (d) Malaysian regulatory framework (3 marks)</h3>\n<p>The Malaysian corporate governance framework encourages listed companies to identify principal risks and establish appropriate systems for managing them.</p>\n<p>The Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance provides principles and practices supporting board oversight and accountability.</p>\n<p>Bursa Malaysia's governance and risk-management/internal-control requirements also provide relevant expectations for listed issuers.</p>\n<p>XYZ Berhad should therefore strengthen board oversight, ensure material risks and control weaknesses are appropriately communicated, and maintain reliable governance and risk-management processes consistent with applicable requirements.</p>\n<h3>Why this answer could earn high marks</h3>\n<p>The strongest feature is that every recommendation addresses a specific failure.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Identified weakness</th>\n<th>Relevant response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal-audit findings concealed</td>\n<td>Information and communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak board challenge</td>\n<td>Governance and accountability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poor management incentives</td>\n<td>Internal environment and culture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ineffective purchasing controls</td>\n<td>COSO control activities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Written policies ignored</td>\n<td>ISO commitment and implementation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak ongoing review</td>\n<td>Monitoring and continual improvement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The underlying logic is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Governance Weakness → Risk Exposure → Applicable Framework → Corrective Action\"><span>Governance Weakness</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Exposure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Applicable Framework</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Corrective Action</span></div>\n<p>For subjective questions, this is considerably stronger than explaining COSO or ISO in isolation.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - Common mistakes in Topic 3 subjective answers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Mistake 1: Treating governance and management as interchangeable</h3>\n<p>A director overseeing the adequacy of controls is performing a governance function.</p>\n<p>An operations manager implementing a specific control is performing a management function.</p>\n<p>One system provides direction and accountability; the other executes activities.</p>\n<h3>Mistake 2: Claiming that regulatory compliance guarantees strong ERM</h3>\n<p>A business may comply with its financial-reporting obligations while making a poor strategic investment.</p>\n<p>Compliance can support governance, but it does not eliminate strategic or operational uncertainty.</p>\n<h3>Mistake 3: Saying that COSO and ISO 31000 are identical</h3>\n<p>Both support risk management, but the lecture presents different structures:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{COSO 2004} = \\text{Objectives + Components + Organisational Levels} }$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{ISO 31000:2009} = \\text{Principles + Framework + Process} }$$</div>\n<h3>Mistake 4: Treating a control as the entire governance system</h3>\n<p>Dual authorisation is a control activity.</p>\n<p>Who approves that policy, receives reports on breaches and oversees its effectiveness are governance questions.</p>\n<h3>Mistake 5: Assuming a comprehensive written policy means risk management is effective</h3>\n<p>The policy must influence organisational conduct.</p>\n<p>A company may have an excellent risk-management manual while allowing management to ignore material exposures.</p>\n<p>The framework's implementation is more important than its appearance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 - Topic 3 subjective practice examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>I've prepared a 60-mark practice examination covering the topics most suitable for application, analysis and evaluation.</p>\n<h3>Subjective practice paper</h3>\n<p>6 questions · 60 marks · Suggested duration: 75 minutes</p>\n<p>10 marks</p>\n<p>Select a questionQ1 - Corporate governance and its elements</p>\n<h4>Question 1</h4>\n<p>12 minutes</p>\n<p>A Malaysian public listed company has unreliable financial disclosures, unclear managerial responsibilities and allegations of unethical supplier practices. Using the five elements of corporate governance, analyse the weaknesses and recommend improvements.</p>\n<p>Your exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer</p>\n<p>Suggested marking points</p>\n<p>Correct explanation of five governance elements</p>\n<p>Specific connection to each case issue</p>\n<p>Identification of accountability/transparency problems</p>\n<p>Explanation of regulatory and ethics implications</p>\n<p>Realistic corrective actions and justification</p>\n<h4>Question 2</h4>\n<p>10 minutes</p>\n<p>Explain and critically analyse the two-way relationship between corporate governance and risk management. Illustrate your answer using a company considering a major acquisition.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nAccurate explanation of governance and risk management</p>\n<p>How governance provides oversight and accountability</p>\n<p>How risk information supports board decisions</p>\n<p>Specific acquisition application</p>\n<p>Balanced evaluation</p>\n<h4>Question 3</h4>\n<p>12 minutes</p>\n<p>Discuss the objectives of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002. Compare its role with the Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance and the Malaysian regulatory framework in strengthening corporate accountability.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nSOX historical motivation</p>\n<p>Financial disclosures, controls and accountability</p>\n<p>MCCG principles and role</p>\n<p>SC/Bursa Malaysia institutional context</p>\n<p>Meaningful comparison without treating laws and codes as identical</p>\n<h4>Question 4</h4>\n<p>15 minutes</p>\n<p>A company has a weak risk culture, fails to identify major supplier dependencies, provides inaccurate reports to its board and rarely reviews the effectiveness of controls. Explain the COSO ERM model and apply relevant components to address these weaknesses.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nThree dimensions of COSO</p>\n<p>Correct COSO 2004 component terminology</p>\n<p>Internal environment and event identification</p>\n<p>Information and communication</p>\n<p>Control activities and monitoring</p>\n<p>Evaluation of interconnected implementation</p>\n<h4>Question 5</h4>\n<p>12 minutes</p>\n<p>A manufacturing company copies an unrelated bank's risk-management policy. Its senior executives provide little support, it uses outdated risk data and employees are excluded from risk discussions. Evaluate the weaknesses using ISO 31000:2009 principles and recommend improvements to its risk-management framework.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nISO principles, framework and process</p>\n<p>Tailored principle</p>\n<p>Best available information</p>\n<p>Human/cultural and inclusive principles</p>\n<p>Mandate/commitment, design, implementation and continual improvement</p>\n<h4>Question 6</h4>\n<p>14 minutes</p>\n<p>A company publishes accurate annual financial statements but routinely approves high-risk strategic projects without independent review. Its board relies solely on management presentations and assumes compliance proves risk management is effective. Critically evaluate this claim and recommend an appropriate governance and ERM response.\nYour exam-style answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking criteria Grade my answer\nSuggested marking points\nDifferentiate compliance and broader ERM</p>\n<p>Identify board oversight and information problems</p>\n<p>Explain strategic risk</p>\n<p>Apply COSO or ISO appropriately</p>\n<p>Justify governance improvements and acknowledge limitations</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 - What I would prioritise before the exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Priority</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Required skill</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Corporate governance and five elements</td>\n<td>Explain and apply to failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Governance-RM relationship</td>\n<td>Analyse the two-way relationship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>COSO ERM</td>\n<td>Explain three dimensions and eight components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>ISO 31000</td>\n<td>Explain principles, framework and process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>MCCG and Malaysian regulators</td>\n<td>Explain functions, compare with SOX</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Sarbanes-Oxley Act</td>\n<td>Explain origins, objectives, controls and limitations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Risk governance</td>\n<td>Explain implementation and the seven building blocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium-high</td>\n<td>Board responsibilities</td>\n<td>Distinguish oversight from management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>GRC</td>\n<td>Explain governance/risk/compliance integration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>AS/NZS 4360 and ISO companion documents</td>\n<td>Explain purpose and distinguish them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The strongest revision focus should be COSO, ISO 31000 and corporate governance, because these areas allow examiners to ask both knowledge-based questions and higher-level organisational case analysis.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3s-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 17 - Last-minute memory sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Topic 3 key relationships</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Corporate governance</th>\n<th>Direction, oversight and accountability</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Management</td>\n<td>Implementation and daily operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk governance</td>\n<td>Ensures risk management is embedded in culture, processes and structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governance elements</td>\n<td>Accountability, transparency, regulatory framework, ethics/social responsibility, administrative structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GRC</td>\n<td>Governance + Risk + Compliance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SOX</td>\n<td>US law strengthening financial reporting, controls and accountability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MCCG</td>\n<td>Malaysian corporate governance principles and practices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>COSO</td>\n<td>Objectives x Components x Organisation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO 31000</td>\n<td>Principles + Framework + Process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO 31010</td>\n<td>Risk-assessment techniques</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO Guide 73</td>\n<td>Risk-management vocabulary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AS/NZS 4360</td>\n<td>Generic risk-management process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Board</td>\n<td>Oversees major risks and control arrangements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control activities</td>\n<td>Specific procedures for implementing risk responses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The most important distinction for your subjective examination</h3>\n<p>Consider this hypothetical situation:</p>\n<p>A company experiences a major financial-reporting scandal. Management concealed important transactions, the board failed to challenge the information it received, internal controls were ineffective and risks were not reported appropriately.</p>\n<p>A basic answer would identify:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The company faces fraud and governance risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A stronger answer would explain:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The scandal illustrates weaknesses in corporate governance, particularly transparency, accountability and board oversight. It also indicates failures in COSO's control activities and information-and-communication components. Management should strengthen segregation of duties, independent reviews and reporting procedures, while the board should ensure material risk information is communicated and acted upon. Applying ISO 31000 principles would additionally help integrate risk management into organisational processes and promote continual improvement.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That answer connects:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Governance Principles → Identified Weaknesses → COSO/ISO Application → Corrective Actions → Long-term Value\"><span>Governance Principles</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Identified Weaknesses</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>COSO/ISO Application</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Corrective Actions</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Long-term Value</span></div>\n<p>My recommended starting question is the COSO framework application (Question 4, 12 marks) in the practice examination. If you can explain why each relevant COSO component addresses a specific weakness-and distinguish those functions from ISO 31000's principles and framework-you will be well prepared for the chapter's more difficult subjective questions.</p></div></details></div></details>");
